is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dswa1, dswa2
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The iptables/nftables `filter/FORWARD` chain on both Office A distribution switches (dswa1 and dswa2), applied on ingress interface `br0.10` (VLAN 10, the 10.1.0.0/24 Office A subnet).

**Mechanism:** Both switches carry a two-rule ACL that first explicitly **accepts ICMP** from 10.1.0.0/24 to 10.3.0.0/24, then immediately **drops all other traffic** (TCP, UDP, etc.) for the same source/destination pair. Because the ICMP-accept rule fires first, `ping` from any Office A host to any Office B host (10.3.0.0/24) succeeds normally, creating the false appearance that the path is healthy. Any attempt to use TCP-based file sharing (SMB, SCP, NFS, rsync) or UDP services from Office A toward Office B is silently discarded at the distribution switch, with no ICMP Unreachable returned to the sender.

**Localization:** dswa1 (`nft filter/FORWARD`, confirmed: 7 accepted ICMP packets from live test, 0 dropped TCP); dswa2 (identical rule set, 0 counters because traffic hit dswa1 first in the ECMP path). The rules are present on both switches, closing both uplink paths simultaneously.

**Root cause:** An ACL drop rule was injected on dswa1 and dswa2 that blocks all non-ICMP traffic originating from Office A (10.1.0.0/24) destined for Office B (10.3.0.0/24), while deliberately exempting ICMP to mask the fault from simple ping diagnostics.

**Fix:** Remove the two ACL entries from both dswa1 and dswa2: `iptables -D FORWARD -i br0.10 -s 10.1.0.0/24 -d 10.3.0.0/24 -p icmp -j ACCEPT` and `iptables -D FORWARD -i br0.10 -s 10.1.0.0/24 -d 10.3.0.0/24 -j DROP` (and the equivalent nft rules in `table ip filter chain FORWARD`).
=== END ===